# Map upstream revision

What resolving the broad world proves wrong in settings, and what is repaired there. Every selected map H2 answers for the settings facts and sources it actually consumes.

## Settings revision from map work {#settings-revision-from-map-work}

Map work tests whether settings defines a usable world basis. Report the exact parent defect it exposes: incompatible axes or units, an extent that cannot contain the required places, a route or site interface with no owner, a weather or time state inconsistent with the delivery, or an adopted source whose authority and uncertainty cannot support the claimed world fact. Repair that settings owner before resolving the map again; a private map assumption is not a repair.

`@evidence` names every upstream target repaired and the map decision that exposed it. When this unit exposes no defect, `@evidenceExclude` names the concrete settings frame, extent, features, states, and source basis examined and why they were sufficient for this map unit. A parentless unit, "no change needed", or one answer repeated across map units covers nothing.

Review question: what did resolving this map unit prove wrong in its actual settings parents, or which concrete parent decisions did it test and find sufficient?

Sources: [OGC Topic 2 on spatial reference-system scope and transformations](https://docs.ogc.org/as/18-005r5/18-005r5.html); [NASA on tracing verification findings to the responsible requirement owner](https://www.nasa.gov/reference/systems-engineering-handbook/)
